          # bare comparison literals in its OWN body and isn't rt_/
          # mem_-prefixed -- and as the user pointed out mid-fix,
          # there's no principled way to call ANY PatLang function
          # "not user code", since x64_runtime.patlang is itself
          # ordinary PatLang compiled by this same pipeline. A real fix
          # (a function declaring "my literals are raw bits, exempt me
          # from numeric-tower semantics") is GitHub #33-adjacent
          # scope, not done here. Interim, narrower mitigation instead:
          # never promote a literal whose value EXACTLY matches one of
          # this backend's own known tag/mask constants -- a real
          # user-facing number essentially never coincidentally equals
          # one of these specific values.
          # Real bug found while testing THIS exclusion list itself:
          # PatLang's own literal parser silently CLAMPS an integer
          # literal exceeding i64::MAX to i64::MAX at parse time,
          # rather than promoting it to a real BigInt the way
          # arithmetic OVERFLOW correctly does -- confirmed directly
          # (`print(17293822569102704640)` gives `9223372036854775807`,
          # not the real value). This meant `to_num(instr[2])` could
          # never equal the numerically-correct comparison value for
          # family_mask (which exceeds i64::MAX), so the exclusion
          # silently never matched. Fixed by comparing the literal's
          # own RAW SOURCE TEXT instead of its parsed numeric value --
          # sidesteps this parser bug entirely, and is simpler besides.
          # (Exclusion list + magnitude check now shared with pass 1's
          # x64_collect_string_data via x64_num_const_needs_bigint_
          # promotion -- see GitHub #40.)
          let needs_promotion = x64_num_const_needs_bigint_promotion(instr[2])
          if is_tower_ctor_arg then
            let needs_promotion = false
          end
          if needs_promotion then
            # GitHub #40: a bare `mov rax, <text>` + rt_bigint_from_i64
            # silently corrupted any literal whose bit pattern's top
            # bit is set (e.g. 18446744073709551615, u64::MAX, encodes
            # to the same 64 bits as -1) since rt_bigint_from_i64
